#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: description
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Explains what usage is and how to estimate how long a job that uses a "
"primitive will take to run"
msgstr ""

#. type: Yaml Front Matter Hash Value: title
#, no-wrap
msgid "Workload usage"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<span id=\"usage\"></span>\n"
"Usage represents consumption of the Qiskit Runtime service and is determined by the amount of time a QPU is locked to execute workloads.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Session usage is measured as the elapsed time while the session remains "
"active, because QPU capacity is reserved for the duration of the session, "
"regardless of whether workloads are actively running. See [Session "
"length](/guides/run-jobs-session#session-length) for more information about "
"session status transitions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Batch usage is measured as the cumulative time the QPU is locked to execute "
"all jobs in the batch.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Single job usage is measured as the time the QPU is locked to execute the "
"job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Note that failed or canceled jobs count toward your usage in certain "
"circumstances - see the [Failed and canceled jobs](#failed-job) section for "
"details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For Pay-As-You-Go Plan users, see [Manage cost](/guides/manage-cost) for "
"details on setting a cost limit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"failed-job\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Usage for failed and canceled jobs {#usage-for-failed-and-canceled-jobs}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "When a job is failed or canceled, the reported usage is as follows:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Job or batch mode: If the failure or cancellation occurred because of a "
"system error, the reported usage is zero. For jobs that failed due to user "
"error or when a user canceled a job, the reported usage is any consumption "
"that has occurred up to that point, including the overhead incurred to "
"prepare the QPU to run the job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '* '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Session mode: The reported usage is the wall-clock time the session is "
"active, regardless of the number of jobs that fail or are canceled.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"view-usage\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Query a workload's actual usage {#query-a-workloads-actual-usage}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"After a workload has completed, there are several ways to view its actual "
"usage:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Run [`batch.usage()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-"
"runtime/batch#usage) or "
"[`session.usage()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-"
"runtime/session#usage) in `qiskit-ibm-runtime` 0.30 or later.  If using an "
"older version of `qiskit-ibm-runtime` (>= 0.23 and < 0.30), the usage can be"
" still be found in `session.details()[\"usage_time\"]` and "
"`batch.details()[\"usage_time\"]`.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use [`GET /sessions/{id}`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-"
"runtime/tags/sessions#tags__sessions__operations__GetSessionDetailsExtendedController_getSessionDetails)"
" to see usage for a specific batch or session.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Use [`GET /jobs/{id}`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-"
"runtime/tags/jobs#tags__jobs__operations__GetJobByIdController_getJobById) "
"to see usage for a single job.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"instance-usage\"></span>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "View instance usage {#view-instance-usage}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"You can view an instance's usage on the "
"[Instances](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/instances) page, or, for those "
"with the proper authority, the "
"[Analytics](https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/analytics) page.  Note that the "
"pages might show different usage numbers because they calculate usage "
"differently.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The Instances page shows real-time usage for the last 28 days (rolling), up "
"to the current time on the current day.  The Analytics page usage is re-"
"calculated hourly and includes the last 28 full days; that is, it shows "
"usage from 00:00 28 days ago to today, at the top of the hour.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Estimate usage before submitting a job {#estimate-usage-before-submitting-a-"
"job}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"While getting an accurate local estimation is complicated by the extra "
"operations done for error suppression and mitigation, you can use this "
"baseline formula to get an approximation of estimated usage:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`<per sub-job overhead> + (rep_delay + <circuit length>) * <num "
"executions>`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`<per sub-job overhead>` is an overhead of approximately 2s per sub-job. "
"This includes operations such as loading the payload into control "
"electronics. Your primitive job may be divided into multiple sub-jobs if it "
"is too large for the execution engine to process all at once.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '- '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"`rep_delay` is a [user-"
"customizable](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-ibm-runtime/options-"
"execution-options-v2#rep_delay) option, and the default is given by "
"`backend.default_rep_delay`, which is 250 microseconds on most IBM Quantum "
"backends. Note that lowering `rep_delay` decreases the total QPU execution "
"time, but at the expense of increased state preparation error rate; see the "
"[Dynamic repetition rate execution](/guides/repetition-rate-execution) guide"
" for more information.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"- `<circuit length>` is the total instruction length. Each instruction takes different amount of time on the QPU, so the total length varies from circuit to circuit. A measurement, for example, can take 56 times longer than an `x` gate. `backend.target[<instruction>][<qubit>].duration` can be used to find the exact duration for each instruction. A typical circuit length is likely between 50-100 microseconds. If you are using error suppression or mitigation techniques with the primitives, extra instructions might be inserted into your circuit, which would increase the total circuit length.\n"
"    <Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"    The [experimental `scheduler_timing` option](/guides/visualize-circuit-timing) returns the total circuit time, but this is NOT the time used for billing.\n"
"    </Admonition>\n"
"- `<num executions>` is the total number of circuits times the number of shots, where the circuits are those generated after PUB elements are broadcasted.\n"
"    - If you are using error mitigation techniques with the primitives, extra circuits might be run as part of the mitigation process, which would increase the total number of executions. Additionally, advanced error mitigation techniques such as PEA and PEC come with much higher overhead because they require running circuits for noise learning.\n"
"    - Estimator groups qubit-wise commuting observables, which reduces the number of executions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"If you aren't using any advanced error mitigation techniques or custom "
"`rep_delay`, you can use `2+0.00035*<num executions>` as a quick formula.\n"
msgstr ""

